  "response": "The scan identifies 20 relevant findings across the three requested modules. The recurring problem is that orchestration, mutation, and callback contracts are implicit, so an agent can make a locally plausible change that violates a lifecycle or return protocol.\n\n## `name_behavior_mismatch`\n\nFindings:\n\n- `src/index.ts`\n  - `build` — `crime_00016`\n  - `buildAll` — `crime_00020`\n- `src/rollup.ts`\n  - `getRollupConfig` — `crime_00004`\n  - `buildStart` — `crime_00011`\n\nProposed refactor:\n\n- Keep the public `build` API if compatibility requires it, but make it a thin wrapper around an explicitly imperative internal function such as `runBuild` or `executeBuild`.\n- Rename `buildAll` to `runAllBuilds` or `executeBuildMatrix`.\n- Split `getRollupConfig` into:\n  - `createRollupConfig(options): RollupOptions` for pure configuration construction.\n  - `prepareRollupBuild(...)` for `setSilent`, cleanup, and other mutations.\n- `buildStart` is probably Rollup’s prescribed hook name, so do not rename the hook itself. Extract its mutation into something explicit such as `cleanOutputBeforeBuild()` and have the hook delegate to it.\n\nWhat an agent can get wrong today:\n\n- Call `getRollupConfig` during inspection or validation and unexpectedly delete output files or alter global logging.\n- Invoke `build` or `buildAll` assuming they only construct artifacts, overlooking cleanup, logger creation, watcher setup, or filesystem mutation.\n- “Fix” `buildStart` by renaming it and silently break Rollup’s plugin lifecycle contract.\n- Duplicate cleanup because the side effect is hidden behind a configuration-oriented name.\n\n## `option_bag_junk_drawer`\n\nFinding:\n\n- `src/rollup.ts`\n  - `getRollupConfig` reads at least ten option fields — `crime_00008`\n\nProposed refactor:\n\nIntroduce phase-specific named types instead of passing the full shared options object through every operation:\n\n```ts\ninterface RollupBuildRequest {\n  entries: NormalizedEntry[];\n  formats: OutputFormat[];\n  external: ExternalMatcher;\n}\n\ninterface RollupOutputPolicy {\n  outDir: string;\n  outExtension?: OutputExtension;\n  cjsInterop: boolean;\n}\n\ninterface RollupPreparationPolicy {\n  shouldClean: boolean;\n  isSilent: boolean;\n}\n\ninterface RollupBuildPlan {\n  inputOptions: RollupOptions;\n  outputOptions: OutputOptions[];\n}\n```\n\nThen separate normalization from execution:\n\n```ts\nfunction createRollupBuildPlan(\n  request: RollupBuildRequest,\n  output: RollupOutputPolicy,\n): RollupBuildPlan;\n\nasync function prepareRollupBuild(\n  policy: RollupPreparationPolicy,\n): Promise<void>;\n```\n\nPrefer required properties after normalization. If an option only applies to certain formats or modes, model that with a discriminated union rather than another optional field:\n\n```ts\ntype FormatPolicy =\n  | { format: \"cjs\"; cjsInterop: boolean }\n  | { format: \"esm\" };\n```\n\nWhat an agent can get wrong today:\n\n- Treat an optional property as universally available.\n- Add a new field to the global options type but forget to normalize or forward it here.\n- Pass CLI-level strings or aliases where normalized entries and formats are expected.\n- Change cleanup or output behavior while intending only to alter Rollup configuration.\n- Create invalid combinations such as a CJS-only option attached to every output format.\n\n## `return_shape_roulette`\n\nFindings:\n\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts`\n  - `sveltePlugin` — six observed shapes, `crime_00017`\n  - nested `setup` — six observed shapes, `crime_00018`\n  - anonymous callback — five observed shapes, `crime_00019`\n\nThese findings likely conflate several legitimate esbuild/Svelte protocols: the plugin factory returns a plugin, `onResolve` returns resolve results, `onLoad` returns load results, and compiler warnings have another shape. Those shapes should remain distinct, but their boundaries must become explicit.\n\nProposed refactor:\n\n- Declare `sveltePlugin(...): Plugin`.\n- Name `setup` and type it against esbuild’s setup contract.\n- Replace the large anonymous callback with named, typed handlers such as:\n  - `resolveSvelteImport(args): OnResolveResult | null`\n  - `loadSvelteComponent(args): Promise<OnLoadResult>`\n  - `toEsbuildMessage(warning): PartialMessage`\n- Use the library’s native result types where available rather than inventing a broad local union.\n- If internal code truly consumes multiple variants through one channel, add a discriminator:\n\n```ts\ntype SvelteTransformResult =\n  | { kind: \"compiled\"; code: string; warnings: PartialMessage[] }\n  | { kind: \"skipped\" }\n  | { kind: \"failed\"; errors: PartialMessage[] };\n```\n\n- Normalize compiler output once, then have the esbuild handler return one stable `OnLoadResult` shape. Optional fields are preferable to unrelated anonymous object literals escaping from the same helper.\n\nWhat an agent can get wrong today:\n\n- Return `{ code }` directly from an esbuild `onLoad` handler that expects `{ contents, loader }`.\n- Put diagnostic `{ text, location }` objects into a result’s `errors` or `warnings` incorrectly.\n- Return a resolution-shaped `{ path, namespace }` object from a load callback.\n- Forget whether “no result” means `undefined`, `null`, or an empty object.\n- Add a branch whose object is structurally accepted locally but ignored by esbuild at runtime.\n\n## Supporting structural findings\n\nThe scan also explains why the three contract problems are difficult to correct safely:\n\n- `src/index.ts`: six large-function findings, a 459-line large-file finding, sync I/O in `normalizeOptions`, and high fan-out of 17 imports.\n- `src/rollup.ts`: `getRollupConfig` is 122 lines, the module has seven outgoing imports, and it also contains direct-clock and swallowed-error findings.\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts`: `sveltePlugin`, `setup`, and the anonymous callback are all independently oversized.\n\nThe type/name refactor should therefore proceed in this order:\n\n1. Add characterization tests around public `build`, Rollup cleanup/config generation, and Svelte resolve/load behavior.\n2. Add explicit return and parameter types without changing behavior.\n3. Extract pure configuration and transformation helpers.\n4. Move cleanup, logging, watching, and other effects into explicitly imperative functions.\n5. Rename internal functions; retain compatibility wrappers for exported APIs where necessary.\n6. Re-run typecheck/tests and compare a fresh JSON scan. The targeted findings should disappear because the contracts became explicit—not merely because functions were renamed to evade detectors.\n\nThe highest-value end state is a thin public build facade, a pure typed Rollup plan builder plus a separate executor, and a Svelte plugin composed from individually typed esbuild callbacks.",
